{
	"comment": "Never-worse ratchet over ABSOLUTE uncovered lines and branches, per file. It fails when a file's uncovered count rises above the number here; it never asserts a percentage, because no percentage in this repo means what it looks like -- at 71.2 % line coverage, roughly 41 of 70 deliberate mutations of production code survived all 996 tests, both Pro gates among them. A file that is not listed counts as 0 uncovered, so a new file arrives with its tests or it arrives red. Moving tested code between files can raise one entry while lowering another: that is the case to re-baseline for. Re-measure with `npm run test:coverage && node scripts/release-checks.mjs coverage --write`, in the SAME commit as the change that needs it, so review sees both. THE NUMBERS ARE PLATFORM-DEPENDENT AND THE GATE RUNS ON LINUX. `--write` records what the machine it ran on measured, and a Mac covers more of the platform-branching files (src/local-register.ts, src/local-model-runtime.ts, src/vision-ocr-runtime.ts) than ubuntu-latest does -- so a baseline written on a Mac can fail CI on files nobody touched. It first did on 2026-08-19, the day src/entry.ts entered the measurement and brought those files with it. When CI reports a file as less covered and a local run does not, raise that entry by the delta CI printed: the floor has to be the platform that enforces it. A `--write` on a Mac will offer to LOWER those same three entries again -- that offer is the flaw, not an improvement; leave them where CI put them.",
